#{[17592186064556 :question/problem "Calculate the integral.\n\n$$\n\\int x^{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186064556 :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int x^{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{x^{5}}{5} + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{x^{5}}{5}+C\\right)=x^{4}.\n$$"] [17592186064557 :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int x^{\\frac{2}{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{3x^{5/3}}{5} + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{3 x^{\\frac{5}{3}}}{5}+C\\right)=x^{\\frac{2}{3}}.\n$$"] [17592186064558 :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int 2 \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}y=2\\sin y + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}y}\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}+C\\right)=2 \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}.\n$$"] [17592186064559 :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int - \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}theta=\\cos θ + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}theta}\\left(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}+C\\right)=- \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.\n$$"] [17592186064560 :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int 3 \\sec^{2}{\\left(z \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}z=3\\tan z + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}z}\\left(\\frac{3 \\sin{\\left(z \\right)}}{\\cos{\\left(z \\right)}}+C\\right)=\\frac{3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(z \\right)}}{\\cos^{2}{\\left(z \\right)}} + 3.\n$$"] [17592186064561 :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int 2 \\csc^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}theta=-2\\cot θ + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}theta}\\left(- \\frac{2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}+C\\right)=2 + \\frac{2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}.\n$$"] [17592186064562 :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int - 2 \\cot{\\left(y \\right)} \\csc{\\left(y \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}y=2\\csc y + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}y}\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sin{\\left(y \\right)}}+C\\right)=- \\frac{2 \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}}{\\sin^{2}{\\left(y \\right)}}.\n$$"] [17592186064563 :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int - 3 \\tan{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\sec{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}theta=-3\\sec θ + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}theta}\\left(- \\frac{3}{\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}+C\\right)=- \\frac{3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}.\n$$"] [17592186064627 :question/worked-solution "Move every term to the left, combine like terms, and factor:\n\n$$\nx^{2} - 5 x + 4=1 \\left(x - 1\\right) \\left(x - 4\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nThe zero-product rule gives\n\n$$\nx = 1, 4.\n$$\n\nSubstitution in the original equation confirms each listed root."] [17592186064628 :question/worked-solution "Move every term to the left, combine like terms, and factor:\n\n$$\nz^{2} + 5 z - 14=1 \\left(z + 7\\right) \\left(z - 2\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nThe zero-product rule gives\n\n$$\nz =-7, 2.\n$$\n\nSubstitution in the original equation confirms each listed root."] [17592186064632 :question/worked-solution "Move every term to the left, combine like terms, and factor:\n\n$$\n- z^{2} + 8 z - 15=- \\left(z - 5\\right) \\left(z - 3\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nThe zero-product rule gives\n\n$$\nz = 3, 5.\n$$\n\nSubstitution in the original equation confirms each listed root."] [17592186064633 :question/worked-solution "Move every term to the left, combine like terms, and factor:\n\n$$\n- x^{2} - 4 x + 5=- \\left(x - 1\\right) \\left(x + 5\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nThe zero-product rule gives\n\n$$\nx =-5, 1.\n$$\n\nSubstitution in the original equation confirms each listed root."] [17592186064634 :question/problem "Calculate\n\n$$\n\\int x2^{x}dx\n$$."] [17592186064634 :question/worked-solution "Use integration by parts with $u=x$ and $\\mathrm dv=2^x\\,\\mathrm dx$. Then $\\mathrm du=\\mathrm dx$ and $v=2^x/\\ln2$, so\n$$\n\\int x2^x\\,\\mathrm dx=\\frac{x2^x}{\\ln2}-\\frac1{\\ln2}\\int 2^x\\,\\mathrm dx\n=\\frac{2^x}{\\ln2}\\left(x-\\frac1{\\ln2}\\right)+C.\n$$\nDifferentiating the result gives $x2^x$, which verifies the antiderivative."] [17592186064644 :question/worked-solution "Using $\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dy}\\arcsin y=\\frac1{\\sqrt{1-y^2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\int\\frac9{\\sqrt{1-y^2}}\\,\\mathrm dy=9\\arcsin y+C.\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on $-1<y<1$."] [17592186064645 :question/worked-solution "Using $\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dx}\\arctan x=\\frac1{1+x^2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\int\\frac3{1+x^2}\\,\\mathrm dx=3\\arctan x+C.\n$$"] [17592186064713 :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int \\sqrt[3]{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{3x^{4/3}}{4} + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{3 x^{\\frac{4}{3}}}{4}+C\\right)=\\sqrt[3]{x}.\n$$"] [17592186064722 :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x^{\\frac{2}{3}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=3x^{1/3} + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(3 \\sqrt[3]{x}+C\\right)=\\frac{1}{x^{\\frac{2}{3}}}.\n$$"] [17592186064723 :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x^{\\frac{3}{5}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{5}{2}x^{2/5} + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5 x^{\\frac{2}{5}}}{2}+C\\right)=\\frac{1}{x^{\\frac{3}{5}}}.\n$$"] [17592186064730 :question/worked-solution "The exponential function is its own derivative. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\int3e^x\\,\\mathrm dx=3e^x+C.\n$$"] [17592186064732 :question/worked-solution "For $a>0$ and $a\\ne1$, $\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dx}a^x=a^x\\ln a$. Hence\n\n$$\n\\int4\\cdot5^x\\,\\mathrm dx=\\frac{4\\cdot5^x}{\\ln5}+C.\n$$"] [17592186064733 :question/worked-solution "Integrate each term:\n\n$$\n\\int(2^x-10e^x)\\,\\mathrm dx=\\frac{2^x}{\\ln2}-10e^x+C.\n$$"] [17592186064783 :question/worked-solution "Move every term to the left, combine like terms, and factor:\n\n$$\ny^{2} - 11 y + 30=1 \\left(y - 5\\right) \\left(y - 6\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nThe zero-product rule gives\n\n$$\ny = 5, 6.\n$$\n\nSubstitution in the original equation confirms each listed root."] [17592186064847 :question/worked-solution "The derivative of $\\operatorname{arcsec}x$ is $\\frac1{|x|\\sqrt{x^2-1}}$ on $|x|>1$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\int\\left(\\frac3{|x|\\sqrt{x^2-1}}+12\\right)\\,\\mathrm dx=3\\operatorname{arcsec}x+12x+C.\n$$\n\nThe constant may be chosen independently on the intervals $(-\\infty,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty)$."] [17592186064848 :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int \\left(- 2 \\cot{\\left(x \\right)} + \\csc{\\left(x \\right)}\\right) \\csc{\\left(x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=-\\cot x + 2\\csc x + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(- \\cot{\\left(x \\right)} + 2 \\csc{\\left(x \\right)}+C\\right)=\\cot^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 \\cot{\\left(x \\right)} \\csc{\\left(x \\right)} + 1.\n$$"] [17592186064849 :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int \\left(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 8 \\sec{\\left(x \\right)}\\right) \\sec{\\left(x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\sec x + 8\\tan x + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(8 \\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + \\sec{\\left(x \\right)}+C\\right)=8 \\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} + \\tan{\\left(x \\right)} \\sec{\\left(x \\right)} + 8.\n$$"] [17592186064852 :question/worked-solution "Rewrite the numerator as $x^2-4=(x^2+1)-5$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int\\frac{x^2-4}{x^2+1}\\,\\mathrm dx&=\\int\\left(1-\\frac5{x^2+1}\\right)\\,\\mathrm dx\\\\&=x-5\\arctan x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186064889 :question/worked-solution "Integrate term by term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int(2e^x+4^x-2x)\\,\\mathrm dx&=2e^x+\\frac{4^x}{\\ln4}-x^2+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186064961 :question/worked-solution "Move every term to the left, combine like terms, and factor:\n\n$$\nq^{2} + 10 q + 21=1 \\left(q + 3\\right) \\left(q + 7\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nThe zero-product rule gives\n\n$$\nq =-7,-3.\n$$\n\nSubstitution in the original equation confirms each listed root."]}
